  "about": "Senior Cloud Software Engineer with 8+ years building production software that ranges from high-availability cloud services to semiconductor test equipment. I thrive on solving complex technical challenges that require both deep technical expertise and cross-functional collaboration.\n\nCurrently at McAfee, I architect and deliver cloud-native solutions on AWS, designing REST and SOAP APIs that serve millions of users with 99% uptime. I work in C#, Go, and C++, building microservices that handle large-scale data processing and integrate seamlessly with internal and external systems. I own my services end-to-end—from initial architecture through implementation, testing, and production support.\n\nMy approach focuses on three principles:\n• Writing maintainable, scalable code that stands the test of time\n• Building robust APIs with clear contracts that make integration straightforward\n• Collaborating across teams to solve problems that span multiple technical domains\n\nWhat sets my experience apart: Before McAfee, I spent time at Advantest developing software for semiconductor test equipment used by industry leaders like Micron, SanDisk, and Samsung. This background taught me the critical importance of reliability, precision, and understanding the full system—from hardware specifications to user workflows.\n\nI'm continuously learning and adapting. Recently, I've been leveraging AI tools like GitHub Copilot to accelerate development while maintaining code quality. I believe in automated testing, CI/CD, and building systems that are performant, secure, and easy to maintain.\n\n🔧 Technical Expertise: C#/.NET, Go, C/C++, REST/SOAP APIs, AWS (Lambda, SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB, S3), Microservices, CI/CD, Agile\n\n🎯 Areas of Impact: Backend Engineering, Cloud Architecture, API Design, Distributed Systems, High-Availability Services\n\n📍 Based in San Jose, CA\n\nAlways open to connecting with engineers, technical leaders, and teams working on interesting challenges in cloud infrastructure, APIs, or scalable systems. Let's connect!",
